## Icons you can use

Use these icons to mark the important parts of a dashboard or page and the things people can do. Each component page explains how to use its icon, including its colour.

### Home Assistant icons

Use Home Assistant’s own icons, written with the `mdi:` prefix, such as `mdi:door-open`. Choose them with the icon picker in Home Assistant. A newer icon may not be in your version of Home Assistant yet. [Icons in the Home Assistant documentation](https://www.home-assistant.io/docs/frontend/icons/) explains how to find them.

Home Assistant picks an icon for each entity and changes it with the state, such as an open and a closed door. If you set `icon:` on a card or an entity, that one icon replaces the state icons. Set your own icon only when the native one is wrong for the device.

This Tile card shows a floor lamp, so it sets a floor lamp icon. The icon no longer changes between on and off, so the tile’s state in words carries the meaning.

```yaml
type: tile
entity: light.hallway_lamp
name: Hallway lamp
icon: mdi:floor-lamp
```

The Civica lighting scene controls use 24px native icons: `mdi:string-lights`, `mdi:soccer`, `mdi:rugby`, `mdi:halloween` and `mdi:alarm-light-outline`. The scene name beside each icon carries the meaning.

### Icons Civica draws

Civica draws a few icons of its own, each for one job.

| Icon | What it means | Where it is used |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Tick and cross | Do and do not | Beside each item under the “Do” and “Do not” headings in [do and do not lists](../components/do-and-dont-lists.html) |
| Right arrow in a circle | This link takes you to a useful page or view | [Action links](../components/action-links.html) |
| Right chevron | This card takes you to a group of pages | [Primary card with a chevron](../components/cards.html#primary-card-with-a-chevron). It is not in a circle. |
| Magnifying glass | Search | The search button at the top of Civica guidance pages |
| Cog | Settings | The settings button on the Civica lighting card. It has no visible label, so its accessible name is “Settings for” and the room name. |

The search button has the accessible name “Search”. Give any icon-only button a name like this.

Other components, such as pagination, use words instead of icons.

## How to use these icons

Use icons sparingly. Never use one for decoration.

### Use SVG

Draw icons as SVG, not as PNG or another bitmap. SVG stays sharp at any size, loads quickly and can take its colour from the page.

In a Markdown card, Home Assistant removes inline SVG. Use a native icon instead, such as `<ha-icon icon="mdi:alert-octagon"></ha-icon>`.

### Make a new icon

Use an existing Home Assistant icon if there is one. If you must draw one for a Civica card:

- draw it as an SVG with `currentColor`, so it follows light and dark mode
- keep its viewBox and proportions, and line it up with the icons next to it
- hide it from screen readers with `aria-hidden="true"` when the control already has a name
- record its source, licence and purpose
- check it at its real size in light mode, dark mode and high contrast

Do not recolour manufacturer artwork or Civica marks to match.

### Use colour with icons

An icon often changes colour with its state, such as a light that is on, and when people point at it or move focus to it. Give a meaningful icon at least 3:1 contrast with the background in every one of those states, in light and dark mode. [Accessibility](../accessibility/index.html) sets the contrast target, and [Colour](colour.html#bg-col-03) explains how to test combinations.

Pale colours, such as yellow, can be hard to see on a white card. Never rely on colour alone: show the state in words too.

### Change the size of an icon

Keep icons at the size Home Assistant draws them, which is 24px in most cards. Make the control bigger, not the icon.

Give an icon-only control an accessible name and a touch target of at least 44px. A 24px icon can sit in a 44px button. Some native targets are smaller, such as the 36px icon on a Tile card. Make sure people can do the same thing another way, such as from the tile’s more-info dialog.

### Accessibility

Home Assistant hides its icons from screen readers. The name people hear comes from the card or entity name, so give every entity a clear name.

[Test the interface](../accessibility/testing.html#borders-icons-and-attention-counts) lists the icon checks: a meaningful label, one purpose, the right proportions, enough contrast and a target people can use.

## Check icons with your household

Do not judge an icon on its own. Check it in place, on the whole dashboard, at the size people will see it on a phone and on a wall panel.

Ask the people who use the dashboard what each icon means before you tell them. If anyone reads it differently, add or change the label, or use a different icon.
